# Reviewed findings that are not secrets. A scan that always reports the same
# thing stops being read, so each entry names what it is and why it is safe.
#
# The Firebase *web* config (apiKey, authDomain, projectId, storageBucket,
# messagingSenderId, appId) is a public client identifier, not a credential. It
# is compiled into the browser bundle by design and is visible to anyone who
# loads the app. Access is controlled by firestore.rules and by Authentication
# → Authorized domains, not by keeping these values private. gitleaks'
# gcp-api-key rule matches the AIza… shape and cannot distinguish a Firebase
# web key from a genuinely secret server-side GCP key.
#
# This particular hit is historical: src/services/firebase.ts carried the value
# as a fallback default and now reads env vars only, but the commit is still in
# the pack. Rotating the key in the Firebase console would not remove it from
# history, and there is nothing to protect by doing so.
